> but as you know I'm a big fan of soft clocks, and I'm hoping a game as
> long as an extinctionist one will be effectively unfeasible.

While I like the idea of increasing difficulty a good choice is necessary
to be made on what to base that clock. To consider turns alone may be
problematic because the time intervals of more or less experienced players
overlap; with teleportitis or if one doesn't find some essential objects
it may require more turns waiting or working to get it. One may argue that
this should be considered by the player in advance, but I feel that this
would not be the right approach. Further, extinctionist games are very
much depending on the role played (or whether one wished for a non-role
specific artifact in his possession); while my non-wizard extinctionist
required nearly 500k turns AFAIR a wizard extinctionist can be done in far
less than 150k turns, which may be about the number of turns for a very
unlucky game or the game of a less proficient player. Maybe coupling the
turns with the number of monster kills to determine the scaling of such a
clock?

